Return early from ARMT_Convert() for buffers shorter than one 4-byte instruction.

ARMT_Convert() computes lim = data + size - 4 before its first bounds check. The 7z reader
calls the folder's branch filter even when the folder unpacks to 0 bytes, with a NULL output
buffer: a 7z archive whose encoded header is a Copy + ARMT folder of unpack size 0 makes
SzReadAndDecodePackedStreams() call ARMT_Convert(NULL, 0, ...). lim then wraps to just below
the top of the address space, the p > lim exit never fires, and the loop reads from address 1
(ASan SEGV in the oss-fuzz/CI build of 7z_fuzzer, out-of-bounds trap in wasm). Adds
corpus/7z_fuzzer/armt_empty_header.7z (58 bytes, valid CRCs), which the CI's "Run fuzzer
against corpus" step runs: it crashes 7z_fuzzer without the fix and passes with it.
Upstream: 7-Zip 23.01+ rewrote Bra.c and BranchConv_ARMT returns early for size <= 2, so the bug is fixed in 7-Zip itself; lzma-fuzz still vendors the old SDK, and its open PR fancycode/lzma-fuzz#4 (2024-04-16) updates it to 23.01.
Drop this patch once #4 lands or the SDK is updated.

diff --git a/sdk/C/Bra.c b/sdk/C/Bra.c
index aed17e3..657dd24 100644
--- a/sdk/C/Bra.c
+++ b/sdk/C/Bra.c
@@ -66,6 +66,10 @@ SizeT ARMT_Convert(Byte *data, SizeT size, UInt32 ip, int encoding)
   Byte *p;
   const Byte *lim;
   size &= ~(size_t)1;
+  if (size < 4) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+
   p = data;
   lim = data + size - 4;
 
